{"seconds": 35.8, "exit": 0, "final": "Bf4 is clearly the best move. It's a fork of the queen and bishop, and after Qg3+ Kxg3, White wins the queen for a bishop (net +6 material advantage).\n\n**Best move:** Bf4\n\n**Main line:** 1. Bf4 Qg3+ 2. Kxg3 Bxf4+ 3. Kg4 (or Kxf4)\n\n**Motif:** fork\n\n**Explanation:**\n\nThe bishop on d2, when moved, discovers the queen's attack down the d-file toward the d7 bishop and king. But the key is that moving it to f4 also creates a fork, attacking both the queen on h2 and the bishop on e5 simultaneously. Black's queen must try to create counterplay with checks (Qg3+), but after Kxg3, White captures the queen for only a bishop\u2014a decisive material advantage of about 6 points. This is a classic example of spotting forks when your pieces move to new squares: always ask what two enemy pieces your piece now attacks.\n\n```json\n{\"move\": \"Bf4\", \"line\": [\"Bf4\", \"Qg3+\", \"Kxg3\", \"Bxf4+\", \"Kg4\", \"Bd6\"], \"motifs\": [\"fork\"]}\n```", "tool_calls": [{"tool": "skill", "args": {"skill": "chess-coach"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-board", "args": {"fen": "r3k1nr/p2b1ppp/2p5/3pb3/4P3/P4B2/1PPBNKPq/R2Q1R2 w kq - 1 20"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-threats", "args": {"fen": "r3k1nr/p2b1ppp/2p5/3pb3/4P3/P4B2/1PPBNKPq/R2Q1R2 w kq - 1 20"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "r3k1nr/p2b1ppp/2p5/3pb3/4P3/P4B2/1PPBNKPq/R2Q1R2 w kq - 1 20", "square": "d1"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "r3k1nr/p2b1ppp/2p5/3pb3/4P3/P4B2/1PPBNKPq/R2Q1R2 w kq - 1 20", "square": "f1"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "r3k1nr/p2b1ppp/2p5/3pb3/4P3/P4B2/1PPBNKPq/R2Q1R2 w kq - 1 20", "square": "a1"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "r3k1nr/p2b1ppp/2p5/3pb3/4P3/P4B2/1PPBNKPq/R2Q1R2 w kq - 1 20", "square": "f3"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "r3k1nr/p2b1ppp/2p5/3pb3/4P3/P4B2/1PPBNKPq/R2Q1R2 w kq - 1 20", "square": "d2"}}, {"tool": "chess-eyes-line_view", "args": {"fen": "r3k1nr/